e of the  volunteers helping refugees and soldiers  in the city of vinitza southwest of kiev  now poland meanwhile is preparing for  tens of thousands more migrants to cross  its border from ukraine after fleeing  the conflict there the polish border  guard says nearly 327 thousand people  have crossed into poland already some 73  000 people on monday alone now people  have been streaming across the border  since russia began its invasion of  ukraine the situation in some places has  turned into a humanitarian crisis  they bring everything they can spare  clothes food money as refugees move west  and soldiers east towards the front here  in vinitza and central ukraine people  are organizing aid for both  natalia who doesn't want to give her  last name leaves a youth center in peace  time she's used to organizing outings  and summer camps  now at war she's busy directing  volunteers and sorting donations  i never imagined i'd be doing any of  this i'm learning all the time we are  improving our work every minute we are  getting there  vinitza in central ukraine has so far  been spared heavy fighting although air  raid alarms are frequent  but like everywhere in the country  people here have been under shock since  russia attacked  now everyone wants to contribute a wave  of solidarity is running through society  natalia is out and about around the  clock the nightly curfew is the only  time she's not on site  i'm also definitely not leaving this is  our home that we love  and we're not going anywhere  not only about donating goods many also  contribute their time making camouflage  nets for the army  green and brown clothes are separated  from the donations and cut into pieces  the rest is a lot of handy work  the ukrainian army has been at war for  eight years still lacking many basic  items including protective equipment  and many here have somebody at the front  a husband brother sister  or in natalya's case a 19 year old son  we are in touch i can talk to him once a  day  it's those few minutes that i'm waiting  for all day  being active is also a way for many here  to deal with their worries  as we've heard poland is preparing for  tens of thousands of migrants to come  across the border from ukraine the  polish border guard saying nearly 327  000 people have crossed into poland  already some 73 000 people just on  monday alone the people have been coming  across the border since russia began its  invasion of ukraine the situation in  some places has turned into a  humanitarian crisis  ndw's correspondent max zander is in  poland at the border with ukraine max  high what is the situation for ukrainian  refugees who've been arriving there  right  so for ukrainian refugees who made it  across the border here to poland if one  thing has become apparent over the last  couple of days is that they are not on  their own no matter how hard the  situation here is the polish government  has prepared for this for days and for  weeks and has put a system in place to  help these people here  and let's have a look around at where we  are here right now we're in chemistry  the border town  a lot of the help that comes here uh has  been done has been carried out by  volunteers  who hand out donations as you can see  here um people here in poland but also  people from abroad have come here to  with uh with with donations uh with food  with diapers with blankets warm clothes  uh to uh brace the cold weather and as  you rightfully uh rightly pointed out  more than 300 thousand people are in  poland now um a few more  many more are expected  so all help is needed at the moment  absolutely it looks like a freezing  night there i know max you touched on  the fact that some procedures have been  put in place to make crossing the border  a bit easier for people coming from  ukraine i understand the government is  also talking about simplifying those  further can you tell us a bit more about  what that looks like  right crossing the border is still very  very difficult um hundreds of thousands  of people are fleeing uh from violence  in ukraine at the moment so there are  still long lines of cars at the borders  that are moving very slowly where people  spend hours even days in cars trying to  to to reach the border the train station  on the other side and live  the platforms are full of people people  are really having a hard time getting  onto those trains become very  competitive now one thing what the  ukrainians are doing on the other side  to speed up the process is we've been  hearing that they're stopping they're no  longer uh checking the documentations of  women and children and that is hopefully  going to speed up the process i'm here  on the polish side this is already the  case that anybody coming from ukraine  can come without having extra to go  through any registration or any  administrative process  and what have you been hearing from the  people you've spoken to who have just  crossed that border from ukraine into  poland  well  the situations are very different  naturally but what we've been hearing  from people we've talked to here  they're all very relieved to finally  have made it um people are people are  exhausted uh people are tired and and  just relieved that they made it here um  now um the first people who came here  over the last couple of days uh mainly  were people who had some sort of  connections here in poland amongst the  ukrainian community here now we're  seeing more and more people  who are just fleeing and they have no  real uh sense of orientation so that's  one thing that they have to do like  reassess their situation here and then  and see how they continue  and what can we expect in the coming  days as more and more people undoubtedly  cross into poland  it really depends and nobody really can  answer that question probably probably i  mean the situation is is evolving very  very quickly  on sunday alone we had a hundred  thousand people reaching poland um and  the government is preparing for for many  more  the polish prime minister um said that  he was actually moved by uh by the  outpouring of help uh by by the polish  people here and that poland uh is uh is  doing everything to help the refugees  which is a pretty much uh  uh 180 degree turn from what we've seen  in the past um how poland was dealing  with with people fleeing uh fleeing from  war but in this case the country is  determined uh to take everybody in as  many people as they can  be this one million or  perhaps many more that's an important  point there our correspondent max zander  thank you so much for your reporting  there
 

